WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.158
Rust's type system helps reduce data races
in safe code,

2
00:00:03.238 --> 00:00:06.405
but concurrent and async applications
still need careful design.

3
00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:09.992
Shared state, locks, channels, task
lifetimes, cancellation behavior,

4
00:00:10.072 --> 00:00:13.239
request timeouts, connection pools, file
handles, memory usage,

5
00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:15.864
and queue sizes all affect security and
reliability.

6
00:00:15.944 --> 00:00:17.245
Memory safety is valuable,

7
00:00:17.325 --> 00:00:20.492
but it does not automatically make a
service resilient under load.

8
00:00:20.572 --> 00:00:23.682
Async tasks should have clear ownership,
timeout expectations,

9
00:00:23.762 --> 00:00:25.968
cancellation behavior, and error
reporting.

10
00:00:26.048 --> 00:00:29.441
A background task that never stops, a
request that waits indefinitely,

11
00:00:29.521 --> 00:00:32.349
or a queue that grows without bounds can
harm availability.

12
00:00:32.429 --> 00:00:36.105
A service that accepts unlimited work can
fail even if the code is memory safe.

13
00:00:36.185 --> 00:00:38.957
Resource limits are part of secure
application behavior.

14
00:00:39.037 --> 00:00:42.260
Locks and shared resources need review for
deadlocks, starvation,

15
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.999
priority inversion, and hidden performance
collapse.

16
00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:48.698
Safe Rust can make shared state safer, but
it cannot decide which lock order,

17
00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:51.719
retry strategy, or queue policy is right
for the application.

18
00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:54.853
Those are design decisions that should be
tested and monitored.

19
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:58.666
Backpressure and graceful degradation help
systems fail in controlled ways.

20
00:00:58.746 --> 00:01:01.687
Connection pools, worker limits, bounded
queues, timeouts,

21
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:05.612
and clear rejection behavior can protect
the application and its dependencies.

22
00:01:05.692 --> 00:01:08.520
Security includes availability and
operational control,

23
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.994
so concurrency design should be reviewed
alongside input validation,

24
00:01:12.074 --> 00:01:13.940
error handling, and release behavior.
